WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.420
Queues are designed to move, buffer, and retain data.

2
00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:07.480
That makes message content a security decision.

3
00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:11.740
Messages should contain only the data the consumer needs to perform the workflow.

4
00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:27.520
Avoid placing secrets, access tokens, refresh tokens, private keys, session IDs, authorization headers, cookies, full credentials, broker credentials, private URLs, reset links, or unnecessary personal data in messages.

5
00:00:27.620 --> 00:00:32.420
Once sensitive data enters the queue, it may travel farther than the producer expected.

6
00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:38.780
Prefer references or identifiers when the consumer can fetch data through an authorized service or database call.

7
00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:43.200
This pattern is not free; it can add latency and dependency calls.

8
00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:50.340
But it often keeps sensitive data closer to the system that owns it and lets the consumer enforce current authorization and state.

9
00:00:50.440 --> 00:01:00.680
When payloads must include sensitive fields, the schema should mark those fields and define access control, encryption decisions, retention, redaction, and support handling.

10
00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:05.360
Message payloads may be visible through more paths than normal application data.

11
00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:20.420
Queue administrators, support tools, replay tools, dead-letter queues, worker logs, traces, retry records, analytics systems, and incident response workflows may all expose message data depending on platform design.

12
00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:26.120
A full customer record in a message can become a full customer record in several operational systems.

13
00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:32.300
That is why payload minimization reduces exposure during failures, retries, investigations, and audits.

14
00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:38.500
Worker configuration and secrets should come from approved secret-management mechanisms, not from queued messages.

15
00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:44.920
Do not pass service tokens, database passwords, or authorization headers through the queue as a shortcut.

16
00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:51.260
If a worker needs authority, grant it narrowly through its runtime identity or secret configuration.

17
00:01:51.360 --> 00:02:01.840
Safe queue design assumes messages may be inspected, retained, retried, replayed, and logged, so publish only what the workflow truly needs.
